                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 1024,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 836,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Collapses a tensor of any shape into one long row, reading it row by row.\n\nt.flatten and t.ravel are two names for the same node: they give the same numbers in the same order, for every tensor.\n\nUse it when a node wants a plain list of values - to plot every value of a grid as one line, to send an image's pixels as a single list, or to turn the result of a 2-D calculation back into a 1-D signal. Seven rows of six become one row of 42.\n\nTHE ORDER IS THE READING ORDER, NOT THE MEMORY ORDER:\nRow 0 first, left to right, then row 1, and so on - as the tensor would print. A tensor that has been turned with t.t is read in its NEW orientation, so its strip runs down the original columns. (Where the memory is already in that order it is a view and costs nothing;\notherwise a copy is made.)\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.flatten\nt.ravel\nNo arguments. (torch.flatten's start and end dims are not available\nhere; to flatten only some axes, use t.reshape with -1.)\n\nINPUTS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or numpy array becomes a float32 tensor first). Receiving it makes the node send. A single number comes out as a 1-element tensor.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nA 1-D tensor holding every value, same dtype and device.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe F (7 by 6) flattened is one strip of 42 cells: the top bar is the first six yellow cells, then each row's stem and arm in turn. Below, the F goes through t.t (turned 6 by 7) before t.ravel: the strip now reads the F's COLUMNS - two long yellow runs for the stem, then the arms.\n\nRELATED:\nt.reshape -1 does the same, and t.reshape can then fold it back. t.view -1 does it only without copying. t.squeeze removes only length-1 axes. flatten (np.ravel) is the numpy version.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 820,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Collapses a tensor of any shape into one long row, reading it row by row.\n\nt.flatten and t.ravel are two names for the same node: they give the same numbers in the same order, for every tensor.\n\nUse it when a node wants a plain list of values - to plot every value of a grid as one line, to send an image's pixels as a single list, or to turn the result of a 2-D calculation back into a 1-D signal. Seven rows of six become one row of 42.\n\nTHE ORDER IS THE READING ORDER, NOT THE MEMORY ORDER:\nRow 0 first, left to right, then row 1, and so on - as the tensor would print. A tensor that has been turned with t.t is read in its NEW orientation, so its strip runs down the original columns. (Where the memory is already in that order it is a view and costs nothing;\notherwise a copy is made.)\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.flatten\nt.ravel\nNo arguments. (torch.flatten's start and end dims are not available\nhere; to flatten only some axes, use t.reshape with -1.)\n\nINPUTS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or numpy array becomes a float32 tensor first). Receiving it makes the node send. A single number comes out as a 1-element tensor.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nA 1-D tensor holding every value, same dtype and device.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe F (7 by 6) flattened is one strip of 42 cells: the top bar is the first six yellow cells, then each row's stem and arm in turn. Below, the F goes through t.t (turned 6 by 7) before t.ravel: the strip now reads the F's COLUMNS - two long yellow runs for the stem, then the arms.\n\nRELATED:\nt.reshape -1 does the same, and t.reshape can then fold it back. t.view -1 does it only without copying. t.squeeze removes only length-1 axes. flatten (np.ravel) is the numpy version."
                },
